while(flag){
	System.out.println("Hello world!");
}
```

We know that once the value of `flag` becomes `false`, the loop will stop executing so why does this code keep on running? Well, the answer lies within the fact that the programmer didn’t write the condition inside the loop correctly. Instead they wrote it outside which makes the condition always evaluate to `true`. If we fix the issue, then the loop stops after just one iteration. So what happens if we remove the condition altogether?

```java
