while(flag){
	System.out.println("Hello world!");
}
```

Well, now the loop never ends and keeps going forever until the JVM crashes. You might think that removing the condition completely defeats the purpose of having loops but that isn’t necessarily the case. Sometimes, developers intentionally leave conditions blank so that people don’t accidently delete them causing bugs.

Another type of control flow obfuscation involves changing the order of operations in a given expression. Consider the following code:

