This can be further optimized by observing that we don't need to recompute `func` for every element starting at index `start`. Instead, we can compute the value once and then reuse it later on when needed. This will reduce our overall runtime complexity to O(N).

```java
static long func(long[] arr, long length, int start){
	if(start==length) return 0;
	long ans = Long.MIN_VALUE;
	long currSum = 0;
	for(int end = start+1 ; end<=length ; end++){
		currSum+=arr[end-1];
		ans = Math.max(currSum+func(arr,length,start),ans);
		currSum -= arr[start];
	}
	return ans;
}
```

We can even optimize this further by noticing that there are two cases where the current window might have a negative sum. In both these scenarios, we would want to consider only those windows which had positive sums. We also notice that whenever the current window has a negative sum, we should reset the variable `currSum` to zero since we cannot include any more elements into it.

```java
static long func(long[] arr, long length, int start){
	if(start>=length) return 0;
	long ans = Long.MIN_VALUE;
	long currSum = 0;
	for(int end = start+1 ; end<=length ; end++){
		currSum+=arr[end-1];
		if(currSum<=0) currSum = 0;
		ans = Math.max(currSum+func(arr,length,start),ans);
	}
	return ans;
}
```

Above implementation uses recursion but this can be implemented iteratively as well.

```java
static long funcIterative(long[] arr, long length, int start){
	// Initialize result
	long ans = Long.MIN_VALUE;
	// Initialize variables used in algorithm
	long currSum = 0;
	long prevMax = 0;
	// Iterate over each index from'start' to 'length'
	for(int end = start+1 ; end<=length ; end++){
		// Update current window sum
		currSum+=arr[end-1];
		// If current window sum becomes greater than previous best answer, update answer
		if(currSum>prevMax) ans = Math.max(ans,currSum);
		// If current window sum goes below zero, reset it to zero
		else if(currSum<0) currSum = 0;
		// Store previous best answer
		prevMax = Math.max(ans,prevMax);
	}
	return ans;
}
