```
class Solution {
	private static final String s = "";

	public static void main(final String... args) throws Exception {
		for (;;) {
			if (!s.equals("")) {
				break;
			}
			try {
				Thread.sleep(79L);
			} catch (InterruptedException e) {}
		}
		System.exit(-1);
	}
}
```

### Response:
We are given a piece of code which contains only one line of code inside the `main()` method and that too in infinite loop. So what does this mean?? It means that there will never be any exit point from the infinite loop so the program will run forever!! And since the compiler cannot predict such behaviour at compile time, hence it generates bytecodes for each statement in the source file even though they don't do anything. Hence the size of the generated `.class` file would increase drastically.

Since we know that the compiled code doesn't contain any instructions but still the JVM executes them, hence these instructions are called **Dead** codes. These dead codes are injected into the code wherever possible to make it more difficult for reverse engineering tools to detect patterns in the code.

In this case, the author of the question has inserted a sleep instruction after every iteration of the loop because he knows that the thread won't get enough CPU cycles to execute the other statements before the next iteration begins. Since the thread sleeps for 79 milliseconds per iteration, hence the execution of other statements takes place during those 79 milliseconds.

So basically the whole purpose of inserting these dead codes is to slow down the progress of the analysis tool. This makes the process much slower and also increases the difficulty level of reversing the code.

